course: dictionary
lesson: 9548
title: Stopcock and the clues around it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A stopcock is a tap or valve on a water pipe. When you turn it, you stop or start the water flowing. «The stopcock is a tap where the water comes into the house, often under the kitchen sink.» The sentence tells you where it is and what it does.
explain: Context clues help you work out the meaning. «If a pipe bursts, tell a grown-up at once; turning off the stopcock or main water valve stops the flood.» A burst pipe and a flood are problems with water, so the stopcock must be the thing that stops the water.
explain: The writer sometimes gives you the meaning straight away. In «turning off the stopcock (the tap that shuts off the house's water)», the words in brackets explain it. Always check: if you swap in «the tap that shuts off the water» and the sentence still makes sense, you are right.
fact: A stopcock is a tap or valve on a water pipe.
fact: «Stopcock» means a tap or valve that stops or starts the flow of water.
fact: In the UK and Ireland, a stopcock is often under the kitchen sink.
fact: In the USA, people more often say «shut-off valve».
fact: Phrases to know: «turn off the stopcock» and «the main stopcock».
exercise: «The stopcock is a tap where the water comes into the house, often under the kitchen sink.» What is a stopcock? | A type of plant | *A tap or valve that stops the water flowing | A kind of kitchen cupboard | A pipe that carries gas
exercise: «If a pipe bursts, tell a grown-up at once.» Why would someone turn off the stopcock? | To play a game | To make the water hotter | *To stop the water and prevent a flood | To make a loud noise
exercise: «...turning off the stopcock or main water valve stops the flood.» Which words in the sentence mean the same as stopcock? | kitchen | pipe | *main water valve | flood
test: «Slight problem in the kitchen, love!» shouted over a burst pipe means a big problem. Where may the water need turning off? | At the front door | *At the stopcock | In the garden | On the roof
test: Which statement about a stopcock is true? | It is only used in the USA | It is a kind of light bulb | It makes water flow faster into the house | *In the UK it is often under the kitchen sink
exam: Dad shouts, «Turn off the stopcock!» What should you do? | Turn on every tap in the house | Call the dog in from the garden | *Find the tap that stops the water and turn it off | Put the kettle on
